From c3a61fd1ac3ecfdc0249a50c10fa964d73ef8ae6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 01:45:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Verify contract-wallet owner signatures through the
 authenticated verifier reader

Under ENCLAVE_CHAIN_AUTHORITY=verifier the Windows network agent has no provider clients, so a
SessionVault / ERC-1271 owner's network policy could only be refused. createNetworkHost now
also uses a verifier-authority LeaseReader (not only a pinned one) for owner verification:
one proof-backed block, the SessionVault factory read from the admin's address book at that
block (a book migration is followed), and any refusal (stale head, missing or bad proof)
answers "not the owner". EOA signatures still need no chain read; the legacy provider path
and the pinned path are unchanged.

The agent config is not given authorityPins: a pinned LeaseReader would read deployments,
registry and connectivity at fixed addresses, which would stop it following the admin book
and drop connectivity for paid networks.

Tests: a unit case on the host consumer, and on canonical reth + Helios the agent's own
construction from the enrollment config (LeaseReader({...cfg.chain}) -> createNetworkHost ->
policyFor) with a real SessionVault owner, admin repoint and restore of the book's factory,
an outsider refused, tampered and missing witnesses refused, and an EOA owner unchanged.
